A||||7||MontanaFiero||How do you remove interior trim?||||5||
Z||000000||MontanaFiero||03-21-2001||05:40 PM||||Specifically, how do you remove the sail panel interior trim, and the rest of the trim that goes around the headliner?  It feels like I am going to break something. The only screw I could find was inside the door jam and I removed the seat belt bolt.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||sqoach||03-21-2001||05:45 PM||||you just take out that one screw in the door jam, and then the two seatbelt bolts. that's all that's actually holding it down. then you hafta be kinda careful and bend the top down. it will flex some but be careful. there are also tabs (two, i believe) that go into the firewall on the rear part of the trim. you just kinda have to work it out of there and be patient. i'm not sure about the top trim. never taken that off before.<P>oh yeah, if i remember correctly, there is another tab that looks just like the door panel tabs. it's holding the top front part of the trim.<BR>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)<p>[This message has been edited by sqoach (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||MontanaFiero||03-21-2001||05:49 PM||||thanks, I am going to trade the notchback upper clip with my parts car. mine is cracked. (what a pain in the a$$, though, having to take the headliner and all the trim off)||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||03-21-2001||07:06 PM||||ya, like he said. the upper seat belt bolt and the long screw in the back door jam is it. it has to come out to get the over the door trim off. it just has 4 clips with teeth and you have to pull n pry and hope the clips pop loose before something breaks, one of lisas broke. I dont know why they have all the one time clips, seems screws would have been a lot better.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MontanaFiero||03-21-2001||08:25 PM||||<B>OK, well that's done.</B> The inside "B" pillar moulding has another "clip" on the front about even with the top seat belt bolt. It needed to be pried out by inserting a screw driver behind the trim and behind the metal part of the clip. (broke the first one) The rest is easy, except for the sail panels themselves. There is a nut on the inside of the "B" pillar holding the corner of the sail panel in place. It is a 11mm nut, deep inside the pillar at an upward angle, and of course, guess where the nut fell.<P>I am considering transferring the donor car's sunroof over too, seems a little scary, though. I read the following posts to help (for anyone else with spring fever who has never tackled this before) <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006166.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006166.html</A>  <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006551.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/006551.html</A> <P>Good practice for when I decide to do the fastback conversion on my SE later this summer.<p>[This message has been edited by MontanaFiero (edited 03-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||webbee||03-21-2001||09:11 PM||||The seal is a caulk like substance. The windshield is glued/caulked on. You can use a piece of wire attached to 2 dowel handles to make a "saw" to cut it out. I had a cracked windshield replaced and they just broke it out, cleaned the old glue out and reglued it back in. <P>I think someone did instructions for replacing both the windshield and sunroof in the archives or the cave.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GoCartGuy||03-22-2001||01:03 AM||||BTW, the seal/glue on the windshield is pure urethane, comes in a caulk tube.  You can find it at most auto glass stores, and some body shop suppliers. Kinda expensive as far as caulk goes, bout $7.00 a tube, but you shouldn't use more than two to reset a windshield.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT "The GoCart"||||reg||1
Z||000007||AusFiero||03-22-2001||02:14 AM||||Removing Interior Trim.....EASY<BR>If its an earlier Fiero, Fire works everytime.||||reg||6
